Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-28
Subject: Reset scanner global `c' when a fresh scanner takes its first file
License: expat

`scanner.pas` keeps `c' (the scanner's lookahead character) as a
unit-level `var', shared across every `tscannerfile' instance. When
one scanner runs to end-of-file it leaves `c' at #26, and that value
survives into the next scanner.

The first thing `tscannerfile.reload' does is short-circuit on
`(c=#26) and (not assigned(inputfile.next))'. For a freshly created
scanner the new inputfile has no `next', so the leftover #26 makes
the scanner exit before its very first buffer fill. The parser then
sees an empty token stream and reports "Syntax error, UNIT expected
but end of file found" against the unit it just opened.

Reset `c' to #0 in `tscannerfile.firstfile' before calling `reload'
so each scanner starts with a clean lookahead state.

--- a/compiler/scanner.pas
+++ b/compiler/scanner.pas
@@ -1780,6 +1780,12 @@

     procedure tscannerfile.firstfile;
       begin
+      { the scanner's `c' is a unit-level global that survives across
+        scanner instances. If the previous scanner exited at EOF it
+        left c=#26, and reload's first guard then short-circuits this
+        scanner's initial buffer fill. Reset c so the new file gets
+        read normally. }
+        c:=#0;
       { load block }
         if not openinputfile then
           Message1(scan_f_cannot_open_input,inputfile.name^);
